{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-343334",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L,$ $W,$ and $H$ be the length, width, and height of the ice block, respectively, and\n\n- let $V$ be the volume of the ice block.\n\nNote that $H,$ $W,$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ is constant. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L=12\\text{ in},\\, & W=5\\text{ in}, \\\\ H=4\\text{ in},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}=-1\\text{ in/h}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-0.4\\text{ in/h}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the three time-dependent variables, $V,$ $W$ and $H,$ is given by the formula for the volume of ice block:\n\n$$\nV=LWH\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWH$ with respect to $t,$ treating $L$ as a constant, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =L\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(WH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =L[W(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t})+H(\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t})] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =L\\cdot W(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(12)(5)(-0.4)+(12)(4)(-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-24+(-48) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-72.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of ice is decreasing at a rate of $72\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/h}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
